{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>Then Moses set apart three cities beyond the Jordan.</b> Scripture tells you that when Moses set apart these cities of refuge in the land of the children of Gad and the children of Reuben, he spoke to Israel the words of the covenant that he would mention immediately afterward. And even though Moses knew that these cities would not afford refuge until three were set apart in the land of Canaan, nevertheless he wished to exert himself even in half a commandment, in order to add it to the rest of the commandments and righteous deeds that he had done. From here we learn a great rousing concerning the performance of commandments, and here we can contemplate the virtues of the commandments and their power and their immense reward when a person performs them in perfection. And it is possible to say that to this the Rabbis, of blessed memory, alluded in the Midrash when they said on (Exodus 34:1): ״פסל לך״ — 'carve for you' — הפסולת שלך — 'the surplus is yours' — from there Moses became rich. They meant to say that from the commandments that are light in a person's eyes, from there Moses became rich — that is, he ascended to great attainment, for that is the true wealth — and no need to say of those commandments that are the essence of the Torah and the foundation of faith." "<b>Toward the sunrise.</b> The Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: whoever kills a soul is exiled toward the sunrise, for so the first man caused death to all his descendants — what is written of him? (Genesis 3): ״וישכן מקדם לגן עדן״ and so forth; and likewise Cain, who killed Abel, was exiled toward the sunrise, as it says (ibid. 4): ״וישב בארץ נוד קדמת עדן״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:43.522747659Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}